data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetPropertyCalls<T>` → `SetPropertyStatements<T>`

The type was renamed. Your expression tree code must update all references:

```csharp
Expression<Func<SetPropertyStatements<Blog>, SetPropertyStatements<Blog>>> setters =
    s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
if (nameChanged)
{
    var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
    setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyStatements<Blog>, SetPropertyStatements<Blog>>>(
        Expression.Call(
            instance: setters.Body,
            methodName: nameof(SetPropertyStatements<Blog>.SetProperty),
            typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
            arguments: new Expression[]
            {
                Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                    Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
                Expression.Constant("foo")
            }),
        setters.Parameters);
}
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(setters);
```

## 2. Complex Type Column Naming Convention Changed

EF Core 10 now uses the **type name** instead of the **navigation name** for complex property columns by default. If both `ShippingAddress` and `BillingAddress` are of type `Address`, you'll get conflicts.

**Fix** — explicitly set column name prefixes to preserve the old navigation-based naming:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, a => {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
        // ... other properties
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, a => {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
    });
});

// For nested: use explicit column names at each level
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, p => {
        p.ComplexProperty(pay => pay.BillingAddress, a => {
            a.Property(x => x.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

Or apply a blanket convention to restore the old behavior globally:

```csharp
configurationBuilder.Properties().HaveColumnName(/* use navigation path */);
```

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — New `CommandSource` Parameter

The `CommandReaderExecuting` (and related methods) now require a `CommandSource` parameter:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new required parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

The `CommandSource` enum tells you what triggered the command (e.g., `CommandSource.ExecuteUpdate`, `CommandSource.SaveChanges`, `CommandSource.LinqQuery`, etc.), which is useful for filtering your diagnostics.

---

**Summary of changes:**

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| `SetPropertyCalls<T>` | Rename to `SetPropertyStatements<T>` |
| Complex type columns | Add explicit `HasColumnName()` or adopt new naming |
| Diagnostics interface | Add `CommandSource commandSource` parameter |
